  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "andean-folk-music-history-revival-and-transmission",
      "title": "Andean Folk Music — History, Revival, and Transmission",
      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** Latin American Folk | **Region:** latin-andes | **Tier:** 6\n**Prerequisites:** regions-latin-andes-05-dance\n**Tags:** latin, andes, latin-america, history,, revival,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "indigenous revival\n\nAndean Folk Music belongs to the **Latin American Folk** curriculum. Inti-Illimani\n\nThis lesson develops listening precision for **indigenous revival** — connecting repertoire, instruments, dance, and the institutions that transmit music today.\n\n![Traditional musicians](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Fiddle.jpg?width=640)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "compared-with-mexico",
      "title": "Compared with Mexico",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Mexican Folk Music** (`regions-latin-mexico-06-history`) covers the same lesson slot from the anchor perspective. This lesson stresses: **indigenous revival**.\n\n| Topic | Mexico anchor | Andean Folk Music (this lesson) |\n|-------|------------------------------|-------------------------------------|\n| Focus | regional archives… | indigenous revival… |\n\n**Parallel anchor lesson:** `regions-latin-mexico-06-history`\n**Secondary comparison (Ireland):** `regions-celtic-ireland-06-history`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "core-material",
      "title": "Core material",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "indigenous revival\n\nStudy **andean folk music** through listening first. Wikipedia's article on [Music of the Andes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Music_of_the_Andes) orients geography and history; recordings prove how musicians phrase ornaments, tempo, and ensemble balance in real rooms."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "regional-accent-and-social-context",
      "title": "Regional accent and social context",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Tradition lives in **community practice** — festivals, dances, teaching networks, and archives — not only on concert stages. For **Latin American Folk**, ask who leads (voice, fiddle, pipes, percussion), whether dance drives repertoire, and how revival institutions shaped what you hear on modern recordings."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "instruments-and-voices-in-this-lesson",
      "title": "Instruments and voices in this lesson",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Lead voices: **quena, charango, voice**. Ensemble layer: **bombo, guitar**. Compare timbre and role with the anchor unit before assuming techniques transfer unchanged."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "forms-dance-and-rhythm",
      "title": "Forms, dance, and rhythm",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Named forms include **huayno, sikuri**. Dance context: **community festival**. Metre and foot-tap feel are not decorative — they explain why musicians accent certain beats and phrase repeats."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "history-transmission-and-today",
      "title": "History, transmission, and today",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Historical threads: **indigenous revival**. Contemporary scenes: **nueva canción overlap**. Tune repertoire anchor: **El Condor Pasa**. Follow lesson playlists and reading lists for recorded examples."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "listening-habits",
      "title": "Listening habits",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "1. Queue one field or archive recording and one contemporary ensemble track.\n2. Log metre, lead instrument, ornament, and social context in four columns.\n3. Return to the parallel anchor lesson if comparison feels abstract.\n4. Avoid generic world-music compilations when building ear training."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "study-facts",
      "title": "Study facts",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "- indigenous revival\n- Lead instruments: quena, charango, voice\n- Ensemble: bombo, guitar\n- Forms: huayno, sikuri\n- Dance: community festival\n- History: indigenous revival\n- Scenes: nueva canción overlap\n- Repertoire: El Condor Pasa\n- Compared with Mexico: regional accent and social context differ.\n- Revival movements and media shaped twentieth-century practice.\n- Dance rhythm and tune metre are linked in this tradition."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "key-points",
      "title": "Key points",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "- Andean Folk Music is a living tradition with regional accent.\n- Focus: indigenous revival.\n- Compared with Mexico: shared heritage, distinct emphasis.\n- Forms: huayno, sikuri.\n- Dance: community festival.\n- Transmission: indigenous revival."
        }
      ]
    },
